WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Ad Voiceover] At Intel,
we're bringing Tokyo 2020

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.863
to the world in a way they've
never experienced before.

00:00:05.863 --> 00:00:09.001
(dramatic music)

00:00:09.001 --> 00:00:10.383
We will host the world.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.640
- [Narrator] These brands paid

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.320
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.050
to be associated with the Olympics.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:17.420
And all this money goes

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.790
to the International Olympic
Committee or the IOC,

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:24.260
which runs all the marketing
programs for the Olympic Games.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.160
But it takes more than marketing.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.120
The IOC powers one of the
most valuable sporting events

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.490
in the world through media deals.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:32.570
- The International Olympic Committee

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.190
has tried to monetize these games

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:38.440
since the very first Olympics
back in Greece in 1896.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.010
And they've evolved every time

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.360
to get more TV broadcasters, for instance,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.770
and other sponsors.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.320
- [Narrator] So how does the IOC generate

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.760
billions of dollars in
revenue from these rights?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.940
And what do companies get in return?

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.873
This is The Economics of The Olympics.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.210
The IOC generates billions in revenue

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.200
through the sale of broadcast
and sponsorship rights.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.080
It made a total of about $4 billion

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.000
from the 2006 and 2008 games,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.260
over $5 billion from 2010 and 2012,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.970
and almost $6 billion from
the 2014 and 2016 games.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.020
In comparison, FIFA,
which runs the World Cup

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.340
made about $4 billion from
the sale of television rights

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.033
and marketing rights
for the 2014 World Cup.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.670
In London in 1948, the IOC
awarded broadcast rights

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.010
for the first time to the BBC.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.970
Today, the sale of broadcast
rights makes up nearly 75%

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.030
of the IOC's total income.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.800
This year, the Beijing
Olympics is being broadcast

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.280
in more than 220 countries.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.050
China Media Group is
airing the games in China.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.160
Discovery is airing it across Europe,

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.950
and CBC is airing it in Canada.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.260
Since 1997, North American
companies have paid more

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.760
for broadcast rights than the
rest of the world combined,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.690
according to IOC data.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.940
NBC has televised every
Olympics since 2000

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.650
and every Summer Games since 1988.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.700
- I've come to associate NBC

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.410
as being the channel of the Olympics,

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.480
and I'm sure many other
Americans do so as well.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.410
- [Narrator] In 2014, NBC
entered into a $7.75 billion deal

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.610
to secure exclusive broadcast
rights in the US through 2032.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.660
That means the company will pay an average

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.900
of over $1 billion for each Olympics.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.260
The deal covers all media
platforms, including TV,

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.900
internet, and mobile rights,

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.560
and also restricts other
media from broadcasting

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.330
the opening and closing ceremonies

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.040
and athletic competitions.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.430
- When it comes down to getting
these broadcasting rights,

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.480
it comes down to one thing which is money.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.360
- [Narrator] These
multi-year broadcast deals

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.870
include both Summer and Winter Olympics.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.260
For example, NBCUniversal made

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.620
about half a billion
dollars more in revenue

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.550
from the 2016 Summer Olympics

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.100
than it did from the 2014 Winter Olympics,

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.590
according to the company's
financial statements.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:55.910
- The Summer Games are a lot bigger

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:56.966
than the Winter Olympics.

00:02:56.966 --> 00:02:58.520
There's about 200 countries

00:02:58.520 --> 00:02:59.800
that compete in the Summer Games,

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.890
about only 100 in the Winter Games.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.810
There's a quarter of the
athletes in the Winter Olympics

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:06.090
compared with the Summer ones.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.480
- [Narrator] After broadcasting rights,

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.100
brand partnerships are the
next income driver for the IOC.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.810
The Olympic Partner Program,
which was launched in 1985,

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.500
grants exclusive marketing rights

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.710
to a select group of corporate sponsors.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:22.090
There are currently 13 top
tier sponsors in this program.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.759
- So you have companies like
Coca-Cola, been around forever.

00:03:24.759 --> 00:03:26.878
♪ So give me a Coke with a smile ♪

00:03:26.878 --> 00:03:28.534
♪ And I promise ♪

00:03:28.534 --> 00:03:29.437
♪ To pass it along ♪

00:03:29.437 --> 00:03:31.160
- But you also now have tech companies

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.300
like Airbnb and Alibaba in
China who have signed up

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.570
in the past decade for
these multi-year deals

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.840
to be one of the elite sponsors.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.120
- [Ad Voiceover] Alibaba
believes in the power of small.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.170
We are proud to be the worldwide partner

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:46.860
of the Olympic Games.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.010
- [Narrator] According
to the latest IOC data,

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.720
top tier sponsors collectively
paid around $1 billion

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.670
for the 2014 and 2016 Olympics.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.960
So what do these brands get in return?

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.040
- They get to use the Olympic logo

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.430
so the iconic rings, the
five interlaced rings

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.610
are probably the best example,

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.180
but also there's an official logo

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.300
for the Beijing Games and the Tokyo Games,

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.310
and all that, too.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.830
- [Narrator] But buying sponsorship rights

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.530
is just the first step.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:12.880
- They spend a lot of money,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.080
tens of millions of dollars per Olympics

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.570
just to get the rights to
use these Olympic logos.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.160
And then they gotta spend even more money,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.420
maybe triple that, to tell everybody

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.447
that they have these rights
via TV advertising that say,

00:04:26.447 --> 00:04:29.720
"Hey, look, we're helping to
put up these feel good games

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:30.970
that brings the world together."

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.140
Or if you're Coca-Cola,
they might want an athlete

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.550
to go around drinking
a Coke at the Olympics.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.250
And it also costs money
just to put the rings

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:38.660
on a can or a bottle of Coke.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.220
- [Narrator] These brand
partnerships are multi-year deals,

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.700
often spanning four or five Olympics,

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.780
which means the IOC
doesn't have to scramble

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.370
to find a new sponsor every two years.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.640
- Airbnb just a couple of years
ago, signed a 10-year deal.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:52.473
So that'll take them

00:04:52.473 --> 00:04:55.080
through the 2028 Olympics in Los Angeles.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.030
- [Narrator] This year,
several Olympics sponsors

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.020
are keeping a relatively low profile

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:00.850
around the Winter Games in Beijing

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:04.040
due to escalating tensions
between the US and China.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.360
- In a normal Olympic
year, these companies

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.050
that already spent a lot of money

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:09.980
to be a top Olympic sponsor

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:11.537
would be normally be bragging about it.

00:05:11.537 --> 00:05:14.900
The 100-day countdown
is usually a big thing.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.640
- [Narrator] For example,
ahead of the 2018 Games,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.150
Coca-Cola put figure
skating star Nathan Chen

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.740
on a billboard in Times Square

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:24.660
to mark the 100-day
countdown to the games.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:26.600
And Visa, which sponsors athletes

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.550
through its Team Visa program
tweeted about its athletes

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.580
and promoted its wearable payment devices

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.013
in the weeks leading up to the 2018 Games.

00:05:34.013 --> 00:05:35.730
- [Ad Voiceover] You dream of amazing.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.380
We dream of amazing ways to pay.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:40.390
- [Narrator] Companies make
million dollar investments

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:41.910
in these sponsorship deals.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.760
But this year, sponsors may risk

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.710
missing global marketing opportunities

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:47.210
that money bought them.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.590
US officials, some Western lawmakers,

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.370
and human rights activists have said

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.190
Beijing's years long campaign

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.510
a forcible assimilation against
the mostly Muslim minorities

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.500
in Xinjiang amounts to a form of genocide.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.460
Beijing denies the
allegation and has protested

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.550
what it calls attempts
to politicize the games.

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.560
- These top tier Olympic sponsors

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.740
are trying to balance their
business in China on one hand

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.020
with their reputation in the
rest of the world on the other.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.580
And they think the smartest
thing to do right now

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.850
is to just not bring any attention

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.160
to the fact that they're
sponsors outside of China.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.240
- [Narrator] Coca-Cola,
for example, hasn't done

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.420
any major advertising campaigns
related to the Olympics,

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.680
except in China, according
to a company spokesman.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.690
He declined to say why the
company made that decision.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.640
Visa and NBC didn't respond
to requests for comment.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.660
The IOC says it recognizes
and upholds human rights,

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.600
but it takes no position
on the political structure,

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.560
social circumstances, or
human rights standards

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:49.960
in the host country.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.160
The committee says it must remain neutral

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.060
on all global political issues.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.080
Looking ahead, NBC is promoting

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.560
its streaming service, Peacock,

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.000
especially as more and
more people ditch cable,

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.520
and TV ratings for the Olympics

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.710
continue on a downward trend.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.850
In January, Comcast said
over 9 million people

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.330
were paying to watch Peacock.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:12.340
The company said it would
spend $3 billion this year

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:15.660
on content for Peacock, double
what it spent last year.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.050
The Beijing Olympics are
an important opportunity

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.400
for Peacock, which has
been called out by analysts

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.390
as being slow to gain traction.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:23.910
Viewers have also complained

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.040
that Peacock didn't have
enough live coverage

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.240
during the Tokyo Summer Games.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.580
This time around, NBCUniversal said

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.730
the entirety of its Olympic broadcast

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.790
would be available live
on Peacock's premium tiers

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:37.360
for the Beijing Games.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.630
- Next step will be to see

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:43.100
whether these online broadcasting
services like Peacock

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.730
are gonna become a bigger part

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.500
of Olympic deals in the future.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:49.083
(bright music)

